  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "CONCERN",
      "title": "Denial cache key joins `name` and `parent` with `_`, so different resources with underscore-containing UIDs share one entry and receive a wrong deny",
      "description": "`userPermDenialCacheKey` returns `namespace + \".perm_\" + userUID + \"_\" + action + \"_\" + name + \"_\" + parent` (cache.go:31). `name` and `parent` are not escaped, and `_` is a valid character in both. `pkg/util/shortid_generator.go:33` defines `validUIDCharPattern` as `a-zA-Z0-9\\-\\_`, and the dashboard and folder services validate UIDs with it. Two different requests can therefore produce the same key: `name=\"a_b\", parent=\"c\"` and `name=\"a\", parent=\"b_c\"` both yield `<ns>.perm_<uid>_dashboards:read_a_b_c`.\n\nThe path to a wrong result:\n1. `Check` consults the denial cache before any permission lookup (service.go:116-121).\n2. When user U is denied `a_b` in folder `c`, service.go:153-155 stores `true` under that key for 30s.\n3. If U is then checked for dashboard `a` in folder `b_c` (which U may view), the lookup at service.go:117 hits and returns `Allowed: false` without consulting the permission map or the database.\n\nThe second resource is denied for the rest of the TTL, and the entry is re-armed each time the first resource is denied after it expires. The denial path only ever returns deny, so this cannot grant access it should not. It is still an incorrect authorization result. A fix should make the key unambiguous, for example by JSON-encoding the tuple or hashing length-prefixed fields, and add a regression case where one name's key ends in a suffix that matches another name's key.",
      "file_ref": null,
      "category": "correctness",
      "raw_category": null,
      "location": "pkg/services/authz/rbac/cache.go:30-32"
    }
  ],
